  safely, and no one writes a story about a ship that simply went by.
  That quality of quiet success is worth remembering. We live in an age that rewards visible achievement, but the
  lighthouse reminds us that some of the most valuable work is invisible. It consists of a steady beam turning through
  the night, a logbook filled with routine entries, a stair climbed once more before dawn. The Pharos, the Eddystone
  tower, Fresnel's glittering lens, and the keepers who lived beside them all share one purpose: to tell the sea where
  the land is, and to tell the sailor that someone is still watching. A lighthouse does not need to be admired to be
  important. It only needs to keep burning.
